{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CORRECTION — retracting the \"CI gap\" claim in my previous comment\n\nMy previous comment asserted that patchset 1 earned `Verified +1` on a tree carrying a\ncomment-hygiene violation, and suggested \"whatever selection CI uses for that guard did not\nexercise it here\". **That is wrong. Disregard it. There is no CI gap.** Recording the\nretraction here because a wrong finding in the ticket record is worse than a missing one —\nsomeone acts on it.\n\n### What I verified this time, instead of inferring\n\n1. **Patchset 1's tree is clean.** Fetched `refs/changes/93/1993/1` (`c11dd21173f4`) into its\n   own worktree and ran the gate directly: `python scripts/check_comment_hygiene.py` -> **exit\n   0**, and `grep -rn 27b868ba55 src tests` -> **3 occurrences**. The sha was in patchset 1 and\n   the tree passed anyway.\n2. **CI does run the gate**, in the reusable that gates both lanes:\n   `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`, step \"comment-hygiene gate\" ->\n   `python scripts/check_comment_hygiene.py`.\n3. **The guard is BLOCK-scoped, not token-scoped.** Its own header\n   (`scripts/check_comment_hygiene.py:10-14`): a triggering block \"is ACCEPTED when it cites a\n   durable reference: a grouped hex ticket id, a word-triple store alias, an ADR id, or\n   `context: external`\". Confirmed with a controlled A/B probe — the SAME sha in two docstrings:\n\n   ```\n   \"\"\"Commit ``27b868ba55`` moved the send path ...\"\"\"              -> 1 rot-prone reference found\n   \"\"\"Bug e339-9709-15fe-419a: commit ``27b868ba55`` moved ...\"\"\"   -> clean\n   ```\n\n### So what actually failed\n\nPatchset 2 added a NEW block — the `_send` helper's docstring, which did not exist in\npatchset 1 — and I wrote the sha into it with no ticket id, alias, or ADR anywhere in that\nblock. That is why the count went 3 -> 4. The gate failed **correctly, on text that did not\nexist when CI voted**. No selection problem, no inconsistency between lanes.\n\n### What stands\n\nThe edit itself stays: citing `emersed-specific-mutt` instead of the bare sha is what\nAGENTS.md asks for and what the guard's acceptors are built around. Only the justification was\nwrong, not the change.\n\n### The habit that failed\n\nI inferred a gap from \"the guard failed locally on a tree CI passed\" without checking whether\nit was the same tree. It was not — my own new docstring was in it. This is the same error the\ntautology check avoided: I ran the discriminating experiment on the mutation and it paid off,\nand did not run one here. The check that would have settled it — fetch the patchset, run the\nscript — took under a minute.\n\nThe commit message does not carry this claim (verified against `git log -1 --format=%B`), so\nno amend is needed for it; patchset 2 stands as pushed."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787311136071162001,"uuid":"92d9cecf-1da8-4949-a3ab-d3c53d569c89"}